<%- include('partials/header', {title: 'Pipeline as Code — Automated WordPress Deployment'}) %>

<section class="project-hero">
  <a href="/" class="project-back-btn"><i class="fas fa-arrow-left"></i> Back to Portfolio</a>
  <h1>Pipeline as Code — Automated WordPress Deployment</h1>
  <p>Fully automated CI/CD pipeline defined as code using AWS CodePipeline and CloudFormation — reproducible deployments triggered directly from GitHub commits.</p>
  <div class="project-hero-tags">
    <span>CodePipeline</span><span>CloudFormation</span><span>GitHub</span><span>PaC</span><span>CI/CD</span><span>Secrets Manager</span>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="project-details">
  <div class="center">
    <img src="/images/pipeline-as-code.png" alt="Pipeline as Code Architecture" class="project-diagram">
  </div>

  <div class="project-section">
    <h3>Project Overview</h3>
    <p>This project extends WordPress-on-EC2 by introducing <strong>Pipeline as Code (PaC)</strong> — the entire CI/CD pipeline configuration is version-controlled as YAML, achieving complete automation from commit to deployment.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Defined <strong>pipeline configuration declaratively</strong> in <code>codepipeline.yaml</code>.</li>
      <li>Used <strong>CloudFormation</strong> to provision both the pipeline and WordPress stack.</li>
      <li>Integrated <strong>GitHub</strong> for source control and deployment triggers.</li>
      <li>Stored sensitive data in <strong>AWS Secrets Manager</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="project-section alt-bg">
    <h3>Architecture Explanation</h3>
    <div class="architecture-grid">
      <div class="arch-component">
        <h4>🔄 CI/CD Pipeline</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>GitHub</strong> — Source, triggers on commit</li>
          <li><strong>CodePipeline</strong> — Source → Deploy stages</li>
          <li><strong>S3</strong> — Artifact store</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="arch-component">
        <h4>🏗️ Infrastructure</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>CloudFormation</strong> — Provisions all AWS resources</li>
          <li><strong>EC2</strong> — Hosts WordPress web server</li>
          <li><strong>RDS (optional)</strong> — MySQL backend</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="arch-component">
        <h4>🔐 Security</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Secrets Manager</strong> — GitHub token, DB password</li>
          <li><strong>IAM Roles</strong> — Least-privilege per service</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="project-section">
    <h3>Deployment Workflow</h3>
    <div class="steps-grid">
      <div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">1</div><p>Developer commits code → <strong>GitHub webhook</strong> triggers CodePipeline.</p></div>
      <div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">2</div><p><strong>Source stage</strong> pulls latest repo version.</p></div>
      <div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">3</div><p><strong>Deploy stage</strong> runs CloudFormation using <code>wordpress-app.yaml</code>.</p></div>
      <div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">4</div><p>CloudFormation <strong>provisions or updates</strong> EC2 and supporting infrastructure.</p></div>
      <div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">5</div><p>WordPress available at <strong>CloudFormation Output URL</strong> automatically.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="project-section alt-bg">
    <h3>Key Learnings</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Understood <strong>Pipeline as Code</strong> and how it complements IaC.</li>
      <li>Gained experience automating CI/CD pipelines using <strong>CloudFormation</strong>.</li>
      <li>Practiced secure secret handling using <strong>AWS Secrets Manager</strong>.</li>
      <li>Validated how a simple <strong>Git commit triggers full infrastructure redeployment</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="center">
    <a href="/" class="btn-back"><i class="fas fa-arrow-left"></i> Back to Portfolio</a>
  </div>
</div>

